---
---

<section id="motivation" class="py-16 sm:py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      Faster, inspectable,{' '}
      <span class="font-serif italic font-normal normal-case text-[#FF4F18]">and spliceable.</span>
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg sm:text-xl text-ink/80 font-medium">
      I started this project to see how much faster Protobuf's wire format could be on modern CPUs
      (see{' '}
      <a href="/how-it-works" class="doc-link">
        how it works
      </a>
      ), and ended up making it work directly on Go structs and TypeScript objects too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t-2 border-ink divide-y-2 divide-ink/20">
    <!-- 1. INSPECTABLE WITHOUT A SCHEMA -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Decode values without a schema
        </h3>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Protobuf jams strings, byte slices, nested messages, maps, and packed arrays into a single wire type (<code>Wire Type 2</code>), and mixes booleans, unsigned ints, and signed ints together. Without the <code>.proto</code> file, you can't even parse a packet into the right types. OmniBuf uses 16 explicit wire types so a reader can always decode the exact values from raw bytes.
        </p>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          If you also want the string field names on the wire, turning on <strong>Field #0</strong> writes the field-name table once at the top of the packet. For an array or map of nested messages, those child field names are defined only once on the parent field instead of repeating string keys on every item like JSON or MessagePack.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-5 min-w-0">
        <div class="os-window overflow-hidden font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
            <span class="font-bold uppercase text-ink text-xs">
              Inspecting Raw Bytes (No Schema)
            </span>
            <div class="os-stripes"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-ink text-paper p-4 space-y-3">
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3 rounded border border-paper/15">
              <div class="text-[11px] uppercase font-bold text-[#FF4F18] mb-1.5">
                Protobuf (Ambiguous Wire Types)
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>1: 1          <span class="text-paper/55"># true? 1? -1 (sint)? enum?</span>
2: 3          <span class="text-paper/55"># +3 (uint/int)? or -2 (sint)?</span>
3: &#123;"FRA"&#125;    <span class="text-paper/55"># string? bytes? sub-msg?</span>
4: "\x0a\x14" <span class="text-paper/55"># bytes? string? packed array?</span></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3 rounded border border-acid/40">
              <div class="text-[11px] uppercase font-bold text-acid mb-1.5">
                OmniBuf (Explicit Wire Types)
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>1: true       <span class="text-acid/80"># bool (0 value bytes)</span>
2: -2         <span class="text-acid/80"># sint (signed int)</span>
3: "FRA"      <span class="text-acid/80"># string (UTF-8)</span>
4: [10, 20]   <span class="text-acid/80"># array&lt;uint&gt; (count=2)</span></code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. READ OR SPLICE WITHOUT DECODING -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Read, drop, or replace individual fields on raw bytes
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Since every integer and container starts with its byte length, a decoder can skip over
          fields it doesn't care about by adding the byte length and jumping to the next tag. If you
          use the generated <code>Opaque</code> wrapper in a proxy or router, you can read one field
          out of a packet or update a field in place without unpacking the rest of the message or
          dropping unknown fields.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-5 min-w-0">
        <div class="os-window overflow-hidden font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
            <span class="font-bold uppercase text-ink text-xs">
              Skip Unneeded Fields &amp; Splice in Place
            </span>
            <div class="os-stripes"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-ink text-paper p-4">
            <!-- Code example -->
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3 rounded border border-paper/15">
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>view := pb.NodeOpaque(pkt)
city, _ := view.City()      <span class="text-acid/80">// skips #1 and #2</span>
pkt = view.SetActive(false) <span class="text-acid/80">// updates raw bytes</span></code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. PROTOBUF + GRPC OR PLAIN STRUCTS -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Works with your existing .proto files, or just plain structs
        </h3>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          If you already use Protobuf, keep your <code>.proto</code> files and generated structs. OmniBuf is strictly a message serialization format (not a new RPC protocol), and because almost every <a href="https://grpc.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">gRPC</a> and <a href="https://connectrpc.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">Connect</a> implementation decouples transport and service stubs from wire encoding, you can register OmniBuf as a custom codec in a few lines of code.
        </p>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          If you usually reach for JSON because you don't want to maintain <code>.proto</code> files and a code generator, put <code>omnibuf:"1"</code> tags on a <a href="/go#struct-tags" class="doc-link">Go struct</a> or use <code>defineMessage()</code> in <a href="/es#define-message" class="doc-link">TypeScript</a> just like <code>json:"host"</code> tags. You get packets that are less than half the size of JSON and 7x to 11x faster on plain structs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-5 min-w-0">
        <div class="os-window overflow-hidden font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
            <span class="font-bold uppercase text-ink text-xs">
              Two Ways In &rarr; Identical Wire Bytes
            </span>
            <div class="os-stripes"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-ink text-paper p-4 space-y-3">
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3 rounded border border-paper/15">
              <div class="text-[11px] uppercase font-bold text-paper/65 mb-1">
                1. Existing .pb.go Struct (From .proto)
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>pkt, _ := omnibuf.Marshal(&amp;pb.Node&#123;Id: 1, City: "FRA"&#125;)</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3 rounded border border-paper/15">
              <div class="text-[11px] uppercase font-bold text-paper/65 mb-1">
                2. Plain Go Struct (No .proto)
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>type Node struct &#123;
  ID   uint32 `omnibuf:"1"`
  City string `omnibuf:"2"`
&#125;
pkt, _ := omnibuf.Marshal(&amp;Node&#123;ID: 1, City: "FRA"&#125;)</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between px-3 py-2 rounded bg-acid text-ink font-bold text-xs border border-ink">
              <span class="uppercase">Same Wire Output (7B)</span>
              <span>21 03 55 07 46 52 41</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
